{
  "topic_analysis": "The impact of AI on software development is a multifaceted topic that requires a balanced narrative arc: starting with historical context to establish credibility, moving through current practical applications (code generation, testing, documentation), addressing the shifting role of developers, examining risks and limitations, and concluding with a forward-looking perspective. The structure should alternate between concrete examples and conceptual framing to maintain accessibility while delivering technical depth. Each section builds on the previous one—context enables understanding of current tools, which informs the role shift discussion, which naturally surfaces risks, leading to a synthesis in the conclusion.",
  "target_audience": "Mid-to-senior software engineers, engineering managers, and technically literate product leaders who are evaluating AI tooling adoption. They understand SDLC fundamentals, have experimented with Copilot/ChatGPT, and need a nuanced framework to assess strategic implications—not a tutorial. They value specificity over hype, care about maintainability and team dynamics, and are skeptical of both AI maximalism and dismissal.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "section_type": "introduction",
      "description": "Open with a brief historical lens: from autocomplete to LLM-based agents. Frame the central thesis—AI doesn't replace developers; it reshapes the abstraction layer they operate at. Hook with a concrete before/after vignette (e.g., a 2020 vs 2024 debugging session). End with a roadmap sentence previewing the five sections.",
      "style_guide": "Technical but accessible. Use one vivid anecdote, minimal jargon, active voice. No bullet points. ~150 words.",
      "target_length": 150
    },
    {
      "section_type": "current_landscape",
      "description": "Survey the three dominant AI touchpoints in daily workflow: (1) code generation/completion (Copilot, Cursor, Codeium), (2) test generation and bug localization, (3) documentation and knowledge retrieval. For each, cite one specific capability and one observed limitation from recent studies or telemetry (e.g., GitHub's 55% acceptance rate, test hallucination rates). Avoid vendor pitch language.",
      "style_guide": "Evidence-led. Cite numbers with sources inline. Use parallel structure across the three touchpoints. One short comparison table in markdown if it fits naturally. ~300 words.",
      "target_length": 300
    },
    {
      "section_type": "role_transformation",
      "description": "Analyze how the developer's cognitive load shifts: from syntax and boilerplate to architecture, verification, and prompt/intent engineering. Discuss the emerging 'centaur' model—human steering, AI executing—and its implications for onboarding, code review norms, and seniority signaling. Include a mini case study: a 2-person team shipping a feature in 2 days that would've taken 5.",
      "style_guide": "Conceptual but grounded. Introduce one coined term ('intent engineering') and define it immediately. Use the case study as narrative proof, not decoration. ~300 words.",
      "target_length": 300
    },
    {
      "section_type": "risks_and_limitations",
      "description": "Systematically address four risk vectors: (1) silent correctness bugs (plausible but wrong), (2) license/dependency opacity, (3) skill atrophy in junior devs, (4) organizational over-reliance without eval frameworks. For each, propose one concrete mitigation (e.g., 'AI-generated code requires two human reviewers', 'mandatory license scan step in CI').",
      "style_guide": "Sober, specific, action-oriented. No fear-mongering. Each risk gets a mitigation paired inline. Use numbered list for scannability. ~250 words.",
      "target_length": 250
    },
    {
      "section_type": "conclusion",
      "description": "Synthesize: the abstraction layer has moved up; competitive advantage now lies in taste, constraints, and verification speed. End with three heuristic questions engineering leads should ask quarterly: (1) What did we delegate this quarter? (2) What broke silently? (3) What did the team learn?",
      "style_guide": "Reflective, forward-looking, memorable. Three questions as a takeaway artifact. Callback to opening vignette. ~200 words.",
      "target_length": 200
    }
  ]
}

{
  "topic_analysis": "This topic explores how community-owned renewable energy projects (solar cooperatives, wind farms, micro-hydro) are transforming rural economies through job creation, energy sovereignty, revenue retention, and resilience. The narrative arc should move from the problem (rural economic decline, energy dependency) to the solution (community ownership models) to concrete evidence (case studies, data) to broader implications (policy, replicability, future outlook). Journalistic narrative demands strong lead, human-centered storytelling, expert voices, and scene-setting details.",
  "target_audience": "Policy-makers, rural development practitioners, clean energy advocates, cooperative movement organizers, and educated general readers interested in just transition stories. They need concrete evidence, replicable models, and human impact—not just theory.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "section_type": "lead_and_nut_graf",
      "description": "Open with a vivid scene in a specific rural community (e.g., a town hall meeting in Oxford County, Maine, or a solar co-op in Spain's Som Energia). Introduce the central tension: rural areas produce energy but export wealth. End with a clear thesis statement: community ownership flips this dynamic.",
      "style_guide": "Journalistic narrative: sensory details, a named protagonist or voice, tight nut graf (3-4 paragraphs max). No jargon. Hook the reader with stakes, not statistics.",
      "target_length": 200
    },
    {
      "section_type": "context_and_problem",
      "description": "Briefly establish the structural problem: rural depopulation, aging grids, extraction economies (coal, gas, corporate renewables), and energy poverty. Use 1-2 telling data points (e.g., % of rural income spent on energy, % of renewable profits leaving region). Frame as systemic, not anecdotal.",
      "style_guide": "Analytical but accessible. Weave data into narrative flow. Use one expert quote (e.g., USDA economist or Energy Democracy Project researcher) to anchor.",
      "target_length": 250
    },
    {
      "section_type": "model_explainer",
      "description": "Define 'community-owned renewable energy': legal structures (co-ops, B Corps, community benefit societies), governance (one member-one vote), capital sources (community shares, revolving loans, IRA/401k rollovers), and profit distribution (bill credits, dividends, community funds). Contrast with corporate PPAs and absentee ownership.",
      "style_guide": "Clear, jargon-free explanatory journalism. Use a sidebar-style bullet list in prose form. One concrete example (e.g., 350-member co-op raising $2M via $1k shares).",
      "target_length": 200
    },
    {
      "section_type": "case_study_1",
      "description": "Deep-dive into one flagship project (e.g., Isle of Eigg's community wind turbine, Colorado's Delta-Montrose Electric Association solar garden, or Germany's 900+ energy co-ops). Cover: origin story, financing mechanics, governance in practice, measurable outcomes (jobs, $ retained, bills lowered, new services funded). Include 2-3 voiced perspectives (founder, member, skeptic).",
      "style_guide": "Immersive narrative nonfiction. Scene-setting, dialogue, chronological flow. Let numbers emerge from story. Address failures/tensions honestly (e.g., maintenance disputes, grid interconnection delays).",
      "target_length": 350
    },
    {
      "section_type": "case_study_2",
      "description": "Second contrasting case: different tech (micro-hydro vs. solar), different governance (indigenous-led vs. municipal), different geography (Global South vs. North). Example: Malaysia's Sarawak micro-hydro co-ops or Navajo Nation's Kayenta solar. Highlight transferable principles and context-specific adaptations.",
      "style_guide": "Same narrative depth as Case Study 1 but tighter. Emphasize what differs and what rhymes. One cross-cutting insight quoted from a network builder (e.g., REScoop.eu or NREL analyst).",
      "target_length": 250
    },
    {
      "section_type": "impact_analysis",
      "description": "Synthesize patterns across cases: multiplier effects (local contractors, apprenticeships), energy sovereignty (rate-setting, shutoff protection), democratic spillover (co-op members running for office, starting food co-ops), and climate resilience (islanding during grid outages). Cite 2-3 peer-reviewed or gov't studies.",
      "style_guide": "Analytical narrative: each pattern illustrated by a micro-anecdote from earlier cases. Avoid laundry lists. End with a measured caveat (capital access, volunteer burnout, policy hostility).",
      "target_length": 250
    },
    {
      "section_type": "policy_and_scaling",
      "description": "What enables or blocks replication: feed-in tariffs vs. net metering wars, securities law exemptions for community shares, USDA REAP/RAISE grants, EU Renewable Energy Directive Article 22. Name 2-3 levers readers can pull (state legislation, utility commission dockets, IRA direct pay).",
      "style_guide": "Solutions journalism. Concrete, actionable, named levers. One 'field guide' paragraph: 'If you're a county commissioner / co-op organizer / ratepayer, here's your next move.'",
      "target_length": 200
    },
    {
      "section_type": "closing_reflection",
      "description": "Return to the opening community or voice. Show a quiet moment: a dividend check funding a roof repair, a teen apprentice splicing fiber. Zoom out: this is infrastructure as inheritance. End on an open question about scale vs. soul.",
      "style_guide": "Lyric journalistic close. No new data. Emotional resonance through concrete detail. Final sentence lingers.",
      "target_length": 150
    }
  ]
}

{
  "topic_analysis": "This topic bridges two critical workplace challenges: maintaining deep work (focused, cognitively demanding work) in distributed/remote teams. The evidence-based angle demands research-backed practices, not anecdotes. The 1000-word constraint requires ruthless prioritization — each section must deliver a distinct, actionable habit with supporting evidence. Structure should move from individual foundations → team coordination → cultural reinforcement, creating a logical implementation ladder.",
  "target_audience": "Engineering managers, tech leads, and senior ICs in distributed teams who struggle with fragmentation (meetings, Slack, time zones) eroding deep work capacity. They want concrete habits they can pilot this week, not philosophy. They value citations but need them distilled to applicable principles.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "section_type": "introduction",
      "description": "Frame the deep work crisis in distributed teams: context-switching costs, async communication overload, and the 'always on' trap. Cite 1-2 key studies (e.g., Mark et al. on interruption recovery, Microsoft WFH research). Define the stakes: deep work drives innovation/quality; shallow work dominates by default. End with the article's promise: 5 evidence-based habits, each with a 15-minute implementation step.",
      "style_guide": "Punchy, data-led opening. No fluff. One vivid statistic. Immediate credibility.",
      "target_length": 120
    },
    {
      "section_type": "habit",
      "description": "Habit 1: Core Hours with Protected Focus Blocks. Evidence: Maker vs. Manager schedule (Graham), attention residue research (Leroy). Practice: Team agrees on 3-4 hour daily overlap window; individuals block 90-min focus sessions within it. No meetings, no Slack, status async. Implementation: Calendar template + Slack status protocol.",
      "style_guide": "Action-first: name the habit, give the evidence in one sentence, prescribe the practice, give the 15-min setup. No hedging.",
      "target_length": 180
    },
    {
      "section_type": "habit",
      "description": "Habit 2: Async-First Communication Protocol. Evidence: Media richness theory (Daft & Lengel) — match channel to task complexity; Microsoft study showing 250% more async comms in WFH. Practice: Default to written async (issues, docs, Loom videos); sync only for ambiguity resolution or relationship repair. Implement: Decision tree flowchart + 'async by default' team charter clause.",
      "style_guide": "Same action-first template. Contrast sync vs. async with a concrete rule of thumb.",
      "target_length": 180
    },
    {
      "section_type": "habit",
      "description": "Habit 3: Explicit Context Packets for Handoffs. Evidence: Transactive memory systems (Wegner) — distributed cognition fails without shared context; Google's 'readme' culture. Practice: Every task handoff includes: goal, constraints, decisions made, open questions, next action. Stored in shared doc linked from ticket. Implementation: Template + PR description mandate.",
      "style_guide": "Show a mini-template. Make it copy-pasteable. Emphasize time saved downstream.",
      "target_length": 170
    },
    {
      "section_type": "habit",
      "description": "Habit 4: Weekly Deep Work Retrospective. Evidence: Psychological safety + learning behavior (Edmondson); agile retrospective evidence. Practice: 15-min async retro: What fragmented focus? What protected it? One process tweak for next week. Rotating facilitator. Implementation: Notion template + recurring calendar invite.",
      "style_guide": "Frame as continuous improvement, not meeting creep. Give the exact agenda.",
      "target_length": 170
    },
    {
      "section_type": "habit",
      "description": "Habit 5: Leader Signaling — Visible Deep Work Modeling. Evidence: Social learning theory (Bandura); 'shadow of the leader' (Schein). Practice: Leaders publish their focus blocks, share async comms exemplars, publicly decline low-value sync invites. No 'do as I say' — 'see as I do.' Implementation: Leader's public calendar view + monthly 'how I worked' micro-post.",
      "style_guide": "Address the power dynamic directly. Give leaders specific visible behaviors, not mindset advice.",
      "target_length": 180
    }
  ]
}

{
    "topic_analysis": "This topic traces the 30-year evolution of cryptocurrency from its ideological roots in the cypherpunk movement to its current institutionalization via ETFs. The narrative arc spans: 1) the philosophical/technical foundations (1980s-2008), 2) Bitcoin's creation and early adoption (2008-2013), 3) the altcoin/Cambrian explosion and infrastructure building (2013-2017), 4) the ICO boom/bust and regulatory awakening (2017-2020), 5) institutional adoption and DeFi summer (2020-2022), 6) the crypto winter, FTX collapse, and regulatory crackdown (2022-2024), 7) the ETF approval and mainstream legitimization (2024-present). The historical narrative style demands chronological storytelling with character-driven scenes, technological milestones as plot points, and thematic through-lines (privacy vs. compliance, cypherpunk ideals vs. institutional capture).",
    "target_audience": "Educated generalists and tech-adjacent professionals (30-55) who've watched crypto from the sidelines—readers of The Verge, Stratechery, or Matt Levine's Money Stuff. They understand basics (blockchain, mining, wallets) but want the *story*: how a mailing list fantasy became a BlackRock product. They crave narrative coherence over price analysis, and appreciate the ironies (censorship-resistant tech feeding Wall Street's hunger for yield).",
    "sections": [
        {
            "section_type": "introduction",
            "description": "Open with the cypherpunk mailing list (1992-1997): Hughes, May, Gilmore, Finney, Szabo, Dai, Back. Set the scene—Encryption as civil disobedience, 'Cypherpunks write code' manifesto, the failed precursors (DigiCash, b-money, Hashcash, Bit Gold). Establish the core tension: cryptographic freedom vs. state monetary monopoly. End with Satoshi's 2008 white paper drop on the cryptography mailing list—the torch passed.",
            "style_guide": "Atmospheric, scene-setting prose. Use primary voices (Hughes' 'Cypherpunks write code,' Szabo's 'trusted third parties are security holes'). Show don't tell the ideology. 300 words.",
            "target_length": 300
        },
        {
            "section_type": "genesis",
            "description": "Bitcoin's 2009-2013 infancy: the first block, the pizza transaction, Silk Road, the bitcointalk forum culture. Early adopters as characters—Finney, Hearn, Andresen, Nakamoto's disappearance. The 'magical internet money' phase: mining on laptops, tipping in BTC, the first exchanges (Mt. Gox). The narrative pivot: from cypherpunk toy to Silk Road's 'money for enemies of the state.'",
            "style_guide": "Character-driven vignettes. Recreate forum texture (bitcointalk.org threads, IRC logs). Contrast the playfulness (10,000 BTC pizzas) with the gathering seriousness (Wikileaks blockade, Silk Road). 350 words.",
            "target_length": 350
        },
        {
            "section_type": "expansion",
            "description": "The Cambrian explosion (2013-2017): Vitalik's Ethereum white paper, the 'world computer' vision, DAO hack, the ICO gold rush. Enterprise blockchain hype (R3, Hyperledger). The split: Ethereum's 'programmable money' vs. Bitcoin's 'digital gold' hardening (blocksize wars, SegWit, Bcash fork). Infrastructure maturation: Coinbase, Bitfinex, Tether's rise. The space professionalizes—conferences, VC money, 'blockchain not Bitcoin.'",
            "style_guide": "Montage style—rapid scene shifts capturing ferment. Juxtapose Vitalik's Toronto whiteboard with Hong Kong ICO roadshows. Show the ideological fracture lines. 300 words.",
            "target_length": 300
        },
        {
            "section_type": "boom_bust",
            "description": "The 2017-2020 cycle: ICO mania ($20B raised), retail frenzy (Coinbase #1 App Store), the 2018 crash, regulatory dawn (SEC DAO report, FinCEN guidance). Tether/Trouble, Quadriga, PlusToken. The 'crypto winter' builds: devs keep shipping (DeFi primitives—Maker, Compound, Uniswap v1). COVID crash then the great monetary expansion: 'money printer go brrr' meets programmable money. DeFi Summer 2020—yield farming, 'food tokens,' TVL explosion.",
            "style_guide": "Boom/bust rhythm. Frenzy language for 2017-18; quieter, builder-focused tone for 2018-20; accelerating energy for DeFi Summer. Use on-chain metaphors (composability as 'money legos'). 300 words.",
            "target_length": 300
        },
        {
            "section_type": "institutional_turn",
            "description": "2020-2022: The institutional bridge. MicroStrategy/Saylor, Tesla, Paul Tudor Jones, MassMutual. 'Bitcoin is a treasury asset' narrative. Custody solutions (Fidelity, Coinbase Custody). Ethereum's beacon chain merge prep. The cultural clash: cypherpunk anarchists vs. compliance officers. NFT mania as side quest (Beeple, Bored Apes, crypto-punks buying JPEGs). Terra/Luna algorithmic stablecoin hubris.",
            "style_guide": "Tension-focused. Boardroom vs. Discord. Saylor's earnings calls vs. Vitalik's research tweets. Show the money changing the culture—'number go up' becoming institutional mandate. 250 words.",
            "target_length": 250
        },
        {
            "section_type": "crisis_reckoning",
            "description": "2022-2024: The great unwind. Terra/Luna ($40B), 3AC, Celsius, Voyager, BlockFi, then FTX/Alameda—$8B hole, SBF trial, the political donation scandal. Regulatory blitzkrieg: SEC 'regulation by enforcement' (Kraken, Coinbase, Binance, Uniswap), DOJ Tornado Cash sanctions, 'operation chokepoint 2.0' banking de-risking. The industry retrenches: 'build in America' vs. 'offshore everything.' Bitcoin ETF applications pile up at SEC—denials, delays, Grayscale litigation victory.",
            "style_guide": "Dramatic, consequential. Courtroom scenes, bankruptcy dockets, Wells notices. The irony: SBF as the face of crypto to Congress. The Grayscale court win as narrative hinge. 200 words.",
            "target_length": 200
        },
        {
            "section_type": "etf_culmination",
            "description": "January 2024: The approval. 11 spot Bitcoin ETFs launch same day—BlackRock, Fidelity, Ark, Bitwise. $4.6B volume day one. The 'IBIT' ticker. Larry Fink's conversion ('digitized gold'). Ethereum ETFs follow (May 2024). The cypherpunk circle reacts: Finney (died 2014), Szabo (skeptical), Dai (quiet), Back (gone). The philosophical terminus: censorship-resistant money now held by the world's largest asset managers. What the cypherpunks won (code runs, no one stopped it) and what they lost (custody, KYC, 1099-DA).",
            "style_guide": "Reflective, valedictory. Contrast the 1992 mailing list with the 2024 NYSE floor. Use the cypherpunk elders as Greek chorus. End on the open question: protocol vs. product, the next 30 years. 100 words.",
            "target_length": 100
        }
    ]
}

{
  "topic_analysis": "This topic argues for the superiority of simple checklists over elaborate productivity frameworks. The narrative should open with a provocative thesis, dismantle the allure of complex systems with concrete failures, then demonstrate why checklists work through cognitive science and real-world proof. A punchy, opinionated tone demands short sentences, rhetorical questions, and unapologetic assertions. The structure must move from problem → evidence → mechanism → verdict in ~200 words, so each section needs to be razor-thin.",
  "target_audience": "Knowledge workers, developers, and solo founders who've burned hours on Notion workspaces, Obsidian graphs, and GTD workflows—only to finish the day with zero shipped output. They're skeptical of productivity porn, crave evidence over inspiration, and want permission to simplify.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "section_type": "opening_thesis",
      "description": "Lead with a blunt claim: complex systems are procrastination theater. Name the enemy (Notion, Roam, PARA, second brains). End with the checklist as the antidote.",
      "style_guide": "Punchy, declarative, zero hedging. One-sentence paragraphs. Profanity-adjacent intensity allowed. ~35 words.",
      "target_length": 35
    },
    {
      "section_type": "complexity_postmortem",
      "description": "Catalog the specific ways complex systems fail: setup theater, maintenance tax, decision paralysis, false completion dopamine. Use concrete examples (tagging schemas, weekly reviews nobody does).",
      "style_guide": "Rapid-fire bullets or staccato sentences. Visceral verbs (\"rotting,\" \"calcifies,\" \"suffocates\"). No academic distance. ~55 words.",
      "target_length": 55
    },
    {
      "section_type": "checklist_mechanics",
      "description": "Explain why checklists win: externalizes working memory, forces atomic decisions, creates visible progress, survives context switching. Cite Atul Gawande or WHO surgical data as authority anchor.",
      "style_guide": "Authority without reverence. \"Checklists do X. Systems promise Y.\" Contrast-driven. ~50 words.",
      "target_length": 50
    },
    {
      "section_type": "closing_verdict",
      "description": "Final challenge: delete the workspace, write three tasks on a sticky, ship one today. No productivity system required.",
      "style_guide": "Imperative mood. Dismissive of the reader's current tool stack. Mic-drop final sentence. ~30 words.",
      "target_length": 30
    }
  ]
}

{
  "topic_analysis": "This topic requires a comprehensive technical reference guide for building maintainable design systems at scale. The guide should progress logically from foundational concepts through architecture, implementation, governance, and operational maturity. Given the 5000-word target, each major section should be substantial (800-1200 words) with depth appropriate for a reference work. The narrative flow should follow the lifecycle of a design system: strategy & foundations → architecture & token systems → component library development → documentation & tooling → governance & contribution workflows → scaling patterns → measurement & evolution. Each section must provide actionable technical guidance, code-level detail where relevant, and decision frameworks rather than just high-level advice.",
  "target_audience": "Senior frontend engineers, design system engineers, frontend architects, and engineering managers responsible for building and maintaining design systems at organizations with 50+ engineers and multiple product lines. They need battle-tested patterns, architectural decision frameworks, and concrete implementation strategies — not introductory material. They understand tokens, component APIs, CI/CD, and monorepo tooling. They're solving problems like token drift, versioning conflicts, adoption resistance, and platform heterogeneity (web, React Native, iOS, Android).",
  "sections": [
    {
      "section_type": "introduction",
      "description": "Frame the problem space: why design systems fail at scale, what 'maintainable' means in practice (change velocity, backward compatibility, cognitive load), and establish the mental model for the guide — treating the design system as a product with its own users, roadmap, and SLAs. Define success metrics: adoption rate, change lead time, defect escape rate, contributor satisfaction.",
      "style_guide": "Authoritative but not academic. Use concrete failure modes from real organizations (anonymized). Establish terminology precisely: tokens vs. primitives, components vs. patterns, system vs. library. No fluff — every paragraph should convey a decision criterion or architectural principle.",
      "target_length": 800
    },
    {
      "section_type": "foundations",
      "description": "Token architecture as the system's type system. Cover: token taxonomy (color, spacing, typography, motion, elevation, border, opacity, z-index), semantic vs. primitive tokens, token naming conventions that scale across platforms, JSON schema design for tooling consumption, token versioning strategy (semver for tokens?), platform-agnostic representation (Style Dictionary, Figma Tokens, W3C DTCG), and the critical decision: single-source-of-truth vs. platform-specific transforms. Include code examples for token structure and transform pipelines.",
      "style_guide": "Technical reference density. Show actual JSON schemas, transform configs, and TypeScript types. Compare approaches with trade-off tables (e.g., centralized vs. federated token ownership). Address the 'dark mode' and 'brand theming' forcing functions that break naive token models.",
      "target_length": 1000
    },
    {
      "section_type": "component_architecture",
      "description": "Component library architecture for maintainability: API design principles (compound components, slot patterns, render props, headless UI), variant management (CVA vs. props explosion), polymorphic components, style override systems (className vs. sx vs. styled-system), tree-shaking compatibility, bundle size budgets, and the component lifecycle: RFC → RFC implementation → beta → stable → deprecated → removed. Cover monorepo structure (Nx/Turborepo), package boundaries, and internal vs. external API surfaces.",
      "style_guide": "Code-first. Show real component signatures, TypeScript interfaces, and rollout checklists. Include a decision matrix for when to build vs. compose vs. extend. Address the 'escape hatch' problem: how consumers bypass the system safely without forking.",
      "target_length": 1200
    },
    {
      "section_type": "documentation_tooling",
      "description": "Documentation as API reference, not marketing. Cover: automated prop table generation from TS/JSDoc, interactive playground architecture (Storybook vs. custom), visual regression testing (Chromatic, Loki, Playwright), accessibility audit automation, bundle size reporting per component, and documentation versioning aligned with package versions. Address the 'docs as contract' mindset: breaking change detection, migration guides generation, and deprecation timelines in docs.",
      "style_guide": "Practical tooling configurations. Show CI pipelines, Storybook configs, and automated changelog workflows. Emphasize developer experience: time-to-first-success, searchability, and offline capability.",
      "target_length": 800
    },
    {
      "section_type": "governance",
      "description": "Governance model that scales: contribution workflow (RFC → design review → engineering review → implementation → release), ownership models (core team vs. federated), breaking change policy (semver, migration windows, codemods), support tiers (core vs. community components), and the 'system as product' operating model: roadmap planning, capacity allocation, stakeholder communication, and sunset policies. Include RACI matrices and decision logs.",
      "style_guide": "Process as code. Show RFC templates, release checklists, and codemod examples. Be prescriptive about what works at scale vs. what works at 5 engineers. Address the 'design vs. engineering' tension explicitly.",
      "target_length": 900
    },
    {
      "section_type": "scaling_patterns",
      "description": "Patterns for multi-platform, multi-brand, multi-repo scale: platform adapters (React, React Native, Web Components, iOS, Android), brand theming architecture (token overlays, component substitution), micro-frontend integration (Module Federation, import maps), version alignment strategies (lockstep vs. independent versioning), and the migration from centralized to federated ownership. Cover the 'design system as platform' evolution: plugin APIs, extension points, and consumer-owned tokens.",
      "style_guide": "Architectural patterns with concrete integration code. Show wrapper component patterns, token transform chains, and federation agreements. Address the 'long tail' of legacy consumers.",
      "target_length": 900
    },
    {
      "section_type": "measurement_evolution",
      "description": "Measuring system health and driving evolution: adoption metrics (static analysis, runtime telemetry), change impact analysis (blast radius calculation), contributor experience surveys, defect classification (token vs. component vs. docs), and the evolution loop: signal → hypothesis → experiment → rollout → measure. Cover sunsetting components, token deprecation pipelines, and the 'system rewrite' decision framework.",
      "style_guide": "Metrics definitions with query examples (SQL, Splunk, Datadog). Show dashboards conceptually. Frame evolution as risk management. End with a maturity model checklist (Level 1-5) for self-assessment.",
      "target_length": 800
    }
  ]
}

{
  "topic_analysis": "The future of work is a multifaceted topic encompassing technological disruption, shifting labor dynamics, evolving organizational structures, and profound societal implications. A balanced exploration requires examining both optimistic and cautionary perspectives, grounding speculation in current trends, and addressing the human dimension alongside technological and economic forces. The narrative should move from present realities through near-term transitions to long-term possibilities, maintaining a thread of human agency throughout.",
  "target_audience": "Professionals across industries (knowledge workers, managers, leaders), policymakers, educators, and intellectually curious generalists who want a nuanced understanding of how work is transforming. They seek actionable insight, not just speculation, and value evidence-based analysis that acknowledges uncertainty without succumbing to either techno-utopianism or doom-mongering.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "section_type": "introduction",
      "description": "Open with a vivid scene-setter contrasting work today with work 20 years ago, then frame the central tension: technology accelerates while human adaptation lags. Define scope (AI, remote/hybrid models, gig economy, skills obsolescence, policy responses) and signal the balanced stance—neither celebration nor alarm, but rigorous exploration.",
      "style_guide": "Essayistic and inviting. Use one concrete vignette. Avoid jargon. End with a clear roadmap sentence previewing the four main sections.",
      "target_length": 200
    },
    {
      "section_type": "current_landscape",
      "description": "Establish the baseline: what the data shows right now. Cover labor market polarization, the hybrid-work settlement, AI adoption curves (generative tools in knowledge work, automation in logistics/manufacturing), rising contingent labor, and the skills half-life metric. Cite 3–4 reputable sources (WEF, OECD, McKinsey, national stats bureaus).",
      "style_guide": "Analytical but accessible. Lead with one surprising statistic per sub-theme. Use parallel structure for readability. Keep opinion out; this is the evidentiary foundation.",
      "target_length": 350
    },
    {
      "section_type": "drivers_of_change",
      "description": "Unpack the four primary engines reshaping work: (1) Generative AI and agentic systems—capability trajectory and diffusion speed. (2) Demographic inversion—aging workforces in Global North, youth bulges in Global South. (3) Climate & resource constraints—green transition jobs vs. stranded assets. (4) Power rebalancing—union resurgence, platform-worker collectivization, regulatory experimentation (EU AI Act, US state laws, right-to-disconnect). Show how drivers intersect, not just run in parallel.",
      "style_guide": "Systems-thinking tone. Use causal-loop language ('reinforces,' 'accelerates,' 'constrains'). One concise diagram-description in prose (no actual graphic). Balance technological determinism with institutional plasticity.",
      "target_length": 400
    },
    {
      "section_type": "scenarios_and_implications",
      "description": "Present three 2035 scenarios—'Augmented Meritocracy,' 'Fragmented Precariat,' 'Regenerative Rebalance'—each 120 words. Scenarios must differ on two axes: technology diffusion speed and institutional adaptiveness. For each, spell out implications for: income distribution, learning ecosystems, geographic mobility, psychological well-being, and civic cohesion. Explicitly flag wild cards (breakthrough/breakdown events).",
      "style_guide": "Speculative discipline. Scenarios are plausibility probes, not predictions. Use 'In this world…' present tense. Equal analytical weight to each. Close with a comparison table rendered in prose.",
      "target_length": 350
    },
    {
      "section_type": "conclusion",
      "description": "Synthesize: the future of work is not a forecast but a design space. Distill three leverage points—lifelong learning infrastructure, portable benefits architecture, algorithmic accountability regimes—that appear decisive across all scenarios. End with a call to reader agency: the question is not 'what will happen?' but 'what will we choose to build?'",
      "style_guide": "Reflective and forward-leaning. Short sentences. One callback image to the opening vignette. No new evidence. Leave the reader with a question that lingers.",
      "target_length": 200
    }
  ]
}

