{
  "content": "Clinical validation of AI diagnostic systems remains one of the most significant barriers to routine implementation, despite growing algorithmic performance in controlled settings. Validation frameworks must address not only statistical robustness but also clinical utility across heterogeneous patient populations, imaging protocols, and disease prevalences. Retrospective cohort studies, while necessary for initial model development, are insufficient for establishing real-world reliability; prospective, multi-center trials with predefined endpoints are increasingly mandated by regulatory bodies. However, such trials introduce logistical complexity, including site variability in data acquisition, annotation standards, and workflow integration. Statistical validation methods — including confidence interval estimation for sensitivity and specificity across subgroups, calibration assessment, and decision curve analysis — must be supplemented by health economic modeling and human factors evaluation. Clinician trust, alert fatigue, and automation bias represent deployment hurdles that cannot be quantified through area-under-the-curve metrics alone. Post-market surveillance, continuous learning system governance, and predefined retraining triggers are now considered essential components of a complete validation lifecycle, yet standardized protocols for these phases remain underdeveloped.",
